{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1774","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1774","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1774\",\"parent_revision\":\"236e6ba0f46db7a18beeb90087c23cf578790ce2\",\"root_revision\":\"165eb8ac3694a73ba5042bae8a340960acdeaf98f615bf2f206ba7e7849dc36f\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Fact-Check section rewritten 2026-04-14** \u2014 removes orchestrator-invented claims (prior Claims 4 and 5) that were not grounded in the item body. Remaining claims each cite a SOURCE_FILE in the item body or a RT-ICA condition.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 1**: `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` contains a \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section that states `dh:task-worker` is the universal dispatch agent.\\nSOURCE_FILE: #1774 Problem section paragraph 1\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE: Read `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` lines 189-192 \u2014 text reads \\\"`dh:task-worker` is the universal dispatch agent for all dh workflows. It must be used for every agent dispatch \u2014 no exceptions.\\\" and \\\"`general-purpose` must never be dispatched from any dh skill or extension point.\\\"\\nCITATION: `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md:189-221` (accessed 2026-04-14)\\n\\n**Claim 2**: `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:80` states the dispatch rule inline.\\nSOURCE_FILE: #1774 Evidence section paragraph 1\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE: Read line 80 of the file \u2014 text reads \\\"Always dispatch `dh:task-worker` as the `subagent_type`. The `agent:` field in the task YAML is NOT a routing directive for the orchestrator \u2014 it is read internally by `task-worker` via the SAM MCP (`sam_task` action) and passed to `profile_load` to specialize `task-worker`'s behavior.\\\"\\nCITATION: `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:80` (accessed 2026-04-14)\\n\\n**Claim 3**: `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:80` is the ONE place the rule is stated inline.\\nSOURCE_FILE: #1774 Evidence section paragraph 1 (verbatim quote: \\\"is the ONE place where the rule is stated inline at a dispatch step\\\")\\nVERDICT: REFUTED\\nEVIDENCE: Grep of `plugins/development-harness/` for the inline rule pattern found a second occurrence at `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/role-resolution-protocol.md:102` \u2014 text reads \\\"Regardless of what role resolves to, the orchestrator always dispatches `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'`. The resolved agent name is recorded in the task's `agent:` field. `task-worker` reads this field via SAM MCP and calls `profile_load(agent_name=...)` to load specialist behavior internally.\\\"\\nCITATION: `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/role-resolution-protocol.md:102` (accessed 2026-04-14)\\nIMPLICATION: The item's Evidence section statement that there is ONE place is inaccurate \u2014 there are TWO. Update the item Description or Goal when correcting, but this does not change the core ask (inline the rule at every dispatch step).\\n\\n**Claim 6**: Existing test harness at `plugins/development-harness/tests/` is structured for pytest and integrated with CI via `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml`.\\nSOURCE_FILE: #1774 Scope Boundary section bullet \\\"Add a linter or check that catches missing inline discipline at future dispatch steps (a grep-based test in `plugins/development-harness/tests/` that fails CI when a dispatch step lacks the reminder)\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE: Read `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml:209` \u2014 pytest is invoked; `plugins/development-harness/tests/` directory contains `test_*.py` files, `conftest.py`, and organized subdirectories. New test files matching `test_*.py` are auto-discovered.\\nCITATION: `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml:209` and directory listing of `plugins/development-harness/tests/` (accessed 2026-04-14)\\n\\n**Claim 7**: Session #1770 is the origin session for #1774 and involved the dispatch-discipline gap.\\nSOURCE_FILE: #1774 \\\"Session Evidence That The Gap Manifests As Real Violations\\\" section\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE: `backlog_view(selector='#1770')` confirms #1770 exists and its sections_index cross-references #1774.\\nCITATION: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view` result for #1770 (accessed 2026-04-14)\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Removed claims (orchestrator-invented, not grounded in item body)**:\\n\\n- **Prior Claim 4** (\\\"No external-agent direct dispatches exist in the plugin tree under the corrected premise\\\") \u2014 this was an orchestrator synthesis based on the corrected regroom premise, not a claim in the item body. The impact-analyst's enumeration is the authoritative source for dispatch-site counts; fact-check is not the right tool for the question. Removed.\\n\\n- **Prior Claim 5** (\\\"`dh:task-worker` is the only agent type that declares SAM MCP in its frontmatter\\\") \u2014 this was a hypothesis invented by the orchestrator during regroom briefing with no SOURCE_FILE in #1774. The fact-checker's structured input contract (`plugins/development-harness/agents/fact-checker.md` lines 38-46) requires a SOURCE_FILE for every claim; this one had none. The resulting \\\"REFUTED\\\" verdict with comparison-bias interpretation spawned #1815 which was then closed wontfix. Removed from this section. The task-worker.md `tools:` field is intentionally absent to inherit all tools \u2014 standard universal-worker configuration per `.claude/rules/frontmatter-requirements.md`. No gap exists.\\n\\n**Lesson for future regroom orchestration**: Follow the fact-checker's structured input contract (CLAIM + SOURCE_FILE + PRIMARY_SOURCE + VERIFICATION_METHOD + FALSIFICATION_CRITERIA). If a claim has no SOURCE_FILE in the item body, do not send it to the fact-checker. If the orchestrator wants to verify a hypothesis about current codebase state, do so with direct Read/Grep, not via the fact-checker.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Goal**: Inline a short dh:task-worker-wrap reminder at every dispatch step in every dh skill, reference file, agent prompt, and workflow document under plugins/development-harness/ where an external (non-dh:) agent is dispatched OR where a SAM task lifecycle requires task-worker wrapping. Rewrite plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md lines 189-221 Dispatch Pattern section to state the narrower corrected rule (task-worker wraps external agents + generic workers; named dh specialists dispatch directly). Add a grep-based pytest CI guard under plugins/development-harness/tests/ that fails when an external-agent direct dispatch appears without task-worker wrapping.\\n\\n**Date**: 2026-04-14\\n\\n**Conditions**:\\n\\n**Condition 1** \u2014 Current CLAUDE.md Dispatch Pattern section content and wording is known. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: fact-checker Claim 1 VERIFIED \u2014 lines 189-221 state \\\"dh:task-worker is the universal dispatch agent for all dh workflows\\\" and \\\"general-purpose must never be dispatched\\\".\\n\\n**Condition 2** \u2014 Count and location of actual external-agent direct-dispatch violations under corrected premise. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: impact-analyst found 1 VIOLATION at plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:115 (dispatches agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst directly in a Mermaid node; fact-checker grep missed this because the pattern is inside a Mermaid block).\\n\\n**Condition 3** \u2014 Framing-wrong sites enumerated. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: 2 sites \u2014 plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md lines 189-221; plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/role-resolution-protocol.md:102.\\n\\n**Condition 4** \u2014 Ambiguous sites enumerated. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: 2 sites \u2014 plugins/development-harness/agents/swarm-task-planner.md:425-430 (agent-assignment table lists external agents as agent: field values without clarifying they are profile_load targets for task-worker, not direct dispatch targets); plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:80 (unqualified \\\"Always dispatch dh:task-worker as the subagent_type\\\" contradicts correct direct dh specialist dispatches later in the same file).\\n\\n**Condition 5** \u2014 Correct-dispatch sites confirmed. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: 11 sites across forensic-review, complete-implementation, groom-backlog-item, work-backlog-item, implement-feature, close, drift-check, groom-drift, error \u2014 all dispatch named dh specialists directly, which is correct under the corrected premise.\\n\\n**Condition 6** \u2014 task-worker.md frontmatter tools: absence interpretation. **State**: AVAILABLE (corrected). Evidence: Prior regroom assessment flagged this as a defect based on fact-checker REFUTED Claim 5. That assessment was wrong. task-worker.md intentionally omits tools: to inherit all available tools, which is the correct configuration for a universal-worker agent that loads arbitrary specialist profiles via profile_load. Specialized dh agents declare tools: to RESTRICT themselves to their role-specific subset; task-worker needs maximum tool surface by design. .claude/rules/frontmatter-requirements.md lists tools: as optional for agents. Spun-off item #1815 closed wontfix 2026-04-14. NOT a defect, NOT in scope for #1774.\\n\\n**Condition 7** \u2014 Scope boundary. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: Only plugins/development-harness/ \u2014 item body confirms.\\n\\n**Condition 8** \u2014 Test harness structure and CI integration path. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: fact-checker Claim 6 VERIFIED \u2014 .github/workflows/code-quality.yml:209 runs pytest; new test_*.py files auto-integrate. No new workflow file required.\\n\\n**Condition 9** \u2014 Canonical inline reminder phrasing. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: User decision 2026-04-14: option (c) \u2014 write a new canonical form with positive framing and Reason annotation. Resolved form: \\\"Dispatching an external agent (any subagent_type outside the dh: namespace)? Wrap via subagent_type='dh:task-worker' and put the external agent name in the SAM task agent: field \u2014 task-worker reads agent: via SAM MCP and calls profile_load internally. Reason: dh:task-worker is the agent type that carries SAM MCP and backlog MCP access; external agents dispatched directly lack these tools and their task-state updates are silently dropped. Named dh:* specialists dispatch directly \u2014 they already have the MCP access they need.\\\"\\n\\n**Condition 10** \u2014 CI guard grep pattern matches external-agent direct-dispatch shape only. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: Pattern `subagent_type\\\\s*=\\\\s*[\\\"'](?!dh:|general-purpose\\\\b)[a-z0-9_-]+:[a-z0-9_-]+[\\\"']` \u2014 matches any subagent_type= assignment where the namespace is NOT dh and the value is NOT general-purpose. Must also scan inside Mermaid code fences (strip fence markers before matching) to catch the implement-feature/SKILL.md:115 case that plain grep missed.\\n\\n**Condition 11** \u2014 Session evidence, #1770 origin. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: fact-checker Claim 7 VERIFIED \u2014 #1770 exists and cross-references #1774.\\n\\n**Condition 12** \u2014 Groom swarm dispatch-site enumeration is complete. **State**: AVAILABLE. Evidence: impact-analyst walked plugins/development-harness/skills/**/*.md, .../references/**/*.md, and agents/**/*.md. Result: 1 VIOLATION, 2 FRAMING-WRONG, 2 AMBIGUOUS, 11 CORRECT \u2014 audit complete.\\n\\n**Counts**: AVAILABLE 12, DERIVABLE 0, MISSING 0\\n\\n**Decision**: APPROVED\\n\\n**Rationale**: All 12 conditions AVAILABLE. Canonical inline reminder drafted by applying user option-(c) decision. task-worker.md frontmatter flag was investigated, identified as comparison-bias judgment error, and closed \u2014 #1815 closed wontfix. One editorial decision deferred to planning (not blocking grooming): whether to also update the existing inline occurrence at role-resolution-protocol.md:102 to match the new canonical form, or leave it as an older variant.\\n\\n**Changes from prior RT-ICA (all struck 2026-04-13 and 2026-04-14)**: Original premise was wrong (\\\"task-worker is the universal dispatch agent\\\"). Wave 1 swarm re-ran with corrected premise. Violation count dropped from 5 HIGH to 1. Condition 6 corrected after user challenge \u2014 absent tools: field is intentional universal-worker configuration, not a defect. Conditions 10 and 12 added \u2014 CI guard pattern and audit completeness confirmation.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` :: \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section \u2014 currently the sole canonical source of the `dh:task-worker`-always dispatch rule. This change disperses the rule into each dispatch step; the editorial decision (keep/remove this section) determines whether this file is updated or partially removed. | Risk: HIGH | Why: source-of-truth relocation; if skills are updated but CLAUDE.md is not reconciled, two conflicting or partially redundant sources exist, creating future confusion.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` :: Phase 1 (feature-researcher dispatch), Phase 2 (codebase-analyzer dispatch), Phase 3 (design-spec role dispatch), Phase 4 (swarm-task-planner dispatch), Phase 5 (plan-validator dispatch), Phase 6 (dh-context-gathering dispatch) \u2014 all 6 phases name specialist agents directly (`@dh:feature-researcher`, `@dh:codebase-analyzer`, resolved design-spec, `@dh:swarm-task-planner`, `@dh:plan-validator`, `@dh:dh-context-gathering`) in delegation prompt templates without any `subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\"` reminder at the dispatch step. Orchestrators reading this skill have no in-step discipline reminder. Each of the 6 phases needs the inline reminder added at its delegation step. | Risk: HIGH | Why: confirmed real-world violation (session #1770 origin incident); 6 separate dispatch steps all missing the discipline; this is the highest-priority file in scope.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` :: Step 2 \\\"Dispatch @dh:code-reviewer\\\" \u2014 explicitly states `subagent_type=\\\"dh:code-reviewer\\\"` and `Delegate the concrete S6 review work with subagent_type=\\\"dh:code-reviewer\\\"`. This is a confirmed violation \u2014 dispatching specialist directly instead of via `dh:task-worker`. Inline reminder + correction needed at Step 2. | Risk: HIGH | Why: directly contradicts the dispatch rule; orchestrators following this skill will bypass task-worker for every S6 review dispatch.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch/SKILL.md` :: SAM dispatch section \\\"Spawn Workers\\\" \u2014 `Agent(team_name=..., name=..., prompt=...)` example does not specify `subagent_type` at all, leaving orchestrators to infer it. No inline dispatch-discipline reminder. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: omitting `subagent_type` in the template is an ambiguity \u2014 an orchestrator could supply any agent type. Adding the reminder and explicit `subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\"` to the template closes the gap.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/execution/SKILL.md` :: Step 3 \\\"Dispatch to Fresh Session\\\" \u2014 says \\\"Launch the resolved agent in a fresh session\\\" without naming `dh:task-worker` as the dispatch target. Step 2 says \\\"If no language manifest exists, dispatch dh:task-worker\\\" but does not state that the orchestrator always dispatches task-worker regardless of which role resolves. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: an orchestrator following this skill literally could infer it should dispatch the resolved specialist agent directly, bypassing task-worker.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/references/groomer-agent.md` :: \\\"Single Item\\\" and \\\"Multiple Items\\\" templates \u2014 both use `subagent_type: \\\"dh:backlog-item-groomer\\\"` directly, not `dh:task-worker`. This is a known special case (groomer is not a SAM task executor, it is a grooming specialist) but the item scope requires auditing whether this exemption is intentional or a violation needing documentation at the dispatch step. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: the dispatch pattern rule says \\\"every agent dispatch\\\"; if groomer is an intentional exception it must be documented inline; if not, it is a violation.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/plan.md` :: Step 4.2 \u2014 `Skill(skill: \\\"add-new-feature\\\", args: \\\"...\\\")` hand-off without dispatch-discipline reminder. This is a skill invocation (not a direct Agent dispatch), but it is the entry point that leads to the 6 violating dispatch steps in `add-new-feature`. No reminder that the downstream skill must use task-worker. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: item explicitly names this as the gap point from the origin incident; reminder here gives the orchestrator a second chance to notice the rule before entering add-new-feature.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` :: `TeamCreate(team_name: \\\"groom-{item-slug}\\\")` and teammate spawn instructions \u2014 spawns impact-analyst, fact-checker, rtica-assessor, classifier, groomer, alignment-analyst as teammates but does not name `subagent_type` for each. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: the subagent_type for each grooming specialist must be `dh:task-worker`; if orchestrators select agents directly by name this bypasses the dispatch rule.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/role-resolution-protocol.md` :: Line 102 \u2014 \\\"the orchestrator always dispatches `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'`\\\" \u2014 already has the inline dispatch rule stated. This is a COMPLIANT reference that should be verified as still accurate after the refactor and not accidentally removed or contradicted. | Risk: LOW | Why: already correct; just verify it stays consistent with any canonical phrasing change made during the work.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-milestone/references/dispatch-plan-schema.md` :: Line 146 \u2014 \\\"Every item dispatches via `Agent(isolation: 'worktree')`\\\" \u2014 references dispatch pattern for milestone worktree workers. Does not address subagent_type selection within those workers. | Risk: LOW | Why: milestone workers are independent orchestrators (kage-bunshin), not task-worker dispatches; verify whether the item scope boundary includes this or not.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/start.md` :: Lines 29, 42, 56, 57 \u2014 `Skill(skill: \\\"dh:implement-feature\\\", ...)`, `Skill(skill: \\\"dh:add-new-feature\\\", ...)`, `Skill(skill: \\\"dh:complete-implementation\\\", ...)` invocations \u2014 these are skill-level handoffs, not direct Agent dispatches, so the discipline applies at the destination skills, not here. Verify no direct Agent calls present. | Risk: LOW | Why: skill invocations are not dispatch steps; the rule applies to Agent() calls with subagent_type, not to Skill() calls.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/` \u2014 no test file currently exists that grep-checks dispatch steps in skill/reference markdown files for the presence of an inline `dh:task-worker` dispatch-discipline reminder. The acceptance criteria explicitly require a new grep-based CI guard test file here that fails when a dispatch step lacks the reminder. No existing Python test in this directory exercises this constraint (confirmed by grep: no match for `dispatch.discipline`, `task.worker` in a structural-check context, or any test that reads SKILL.md content for dispatch compliance). | Risk: HIGH | Why: without this test, future additions to any skill file can silently re-introduce the violation; the test is the enforcement mechanism named in the ACs.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_agent_profile/test_server.py` :: `test_profile_load_task_worker_returns_nonempty_body` (line 779) \u2014 tests that `profile_load(\\\"task-worker\\\")` returns a non-empty body, confirming the task-worker agent loads correctly. This test validates the downstream agent side of the dispatch pattern but does not test whether orchestrators are instructed to use it. | Risk: LOW | Why: already passes; no change needed; listed for completeness as the only test touching task-worker dispatch behavior.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` :: \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section \u2014 this is the current canonical documentation of the rule. After skills are updated with inline reminders, this section becomes partially redundant. The editorial decision (keep as safety net vs. remove) must be recorded in the PR. Until the decision is made, this section is the backup; after skills are updated, its continued accuracy must be verified against the canonical phrasing adopted in the inline reminders. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: stale or contradictory phrasing between CLAUDE.md and inline reminders creates confusion; the PR must explicitly address this.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` :: \\\"Extension rule\\\" sub-section under Dispatch Pattern \u2014 states \\\"When adding a new dispatch step to any dh skill, reference file, or workflow document: Dispatch `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'` \u2014 always\\\". This guidance is correct but currently the ONLY place it lives; after the refactor it becomes a policy reference rather than the primary enforcement point. | Risk: LOW | Why: accurate today; may need a cross-reference added pointing to the new inline reminders.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` \u2014 the existing CI pipeline runs Python tests via pytest (jobs: lint-python, typecheck-python, test-python implied by the pattern). The new grep-based dispatch-discipline test file under `plugins/development-harness/tests/` will be picked up automatically by the existing pytest configuration if it follows the `test_*.py` naming convention. No new CI workflow job is required, but the test must be verified to run under the existing test job. | Risk: LOW | Why: no CI workflow file needs direct modification; the new test integrates with the existing test runner automatically.\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` :: quality-gate job \u2014 if the new dispatch-discipline test fails, it will fail the existing test job and block the quality gate. This is the intended behavior. | Risk: LOW | Why: existing pattern is correct; no change required.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/task-worker.md` \u2014 describes the universal SAM task executor. Does not currently contain any inbound instruction about how orchestrators must dispatch it. This file itself is correct (it is the receiver, not the dispatcher). No change required unless the canonical inline wording adopted for dispatch steps needs to reference the task-worker agent description for context. | Risk: LOW | Why: task-worker is the target, not the dispatcher; no dispatch instruction lives here.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` \u2014 spawned via `TeamCreate` in the grooming swarm (`swarm.md`). The swarm does not specify `subagent_type` for each teammate. Once the dispatch discipline is applied to `swarm.md`, this agent's dispatch invocation will be governed by the inline reminder added there. | Risk: LOW | Why: no change to the agent file itself; the dispatch instruction change lives in `swarm.md`.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` \u2014 dispatched with `subagent_type: \\\"dh:backlog-item-groomer\\\"` in `groomer-agent.md`. This is the contentious case that needs the editorial decision: is `backlog-item-groomer` exempt from the `dh:task-worker` rule because it is not executing a SAM task? If not exempt, `groomer-agent.md` must be updated. If exempt, the exemption must be documented inline. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: affects whether groomer-agent.md templates are violations or intentional; the decision must be explicit.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` | Role: producer | Connection: defines the dispatch discipline rule; source being dispersed | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: 6 dispatch steps, all without inline reminder; confirmed origin of real violations | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: Step 2 explicitly dispatches `subagent_type=\\\"dh:code-reviewer\\\"` in violation of the rule | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/` (new file to create) | Role: ci | Connection: no grep-based dispatch-discipline guard exists; acceptance criteria require one | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch/SKILL.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: SAM dispatch Agent() template omits subagent_type; no inline reminder | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/execution/SKILL.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: Step 3 says \\\"launch resolved agent\\\" without naming task-worker as mandatory dispatch type | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/references/groomer-agent.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: dispatches `dh:backlog-item-groomer` directly; needs editorial decision on exemption | Action: C