" without naming task-worker as mandatory | Must include inline reminder that dispatch target is always `subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\"` regardless of resolved role | Ambiguous language permits interpreting resolved specialist as the dispatch target; no inline discipline reminder | MEDIUM |\\n| add-new-feature/SKILL.md:91+ | Phase delegation prompts directly reference specialist agents (`@dh:feature-researcher`, `@dh:codebase-analyzer`, etc.) without `subagent_type` or inline task-worker reminder | Each phase delegation must include inline reminder that dispatch target is `subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\"` and specialist is selected via task YAML | All 6 phases (discovery, analysis, design, decomposition, validation, context) lack inline discipline reminder; confirmed origin incident from session #1770 | HIGH |\\n| work-backlog-item/plan.md:14 | Skill invocation to add-new-feature at Step 4.2 lacks dispatch-discipline reminder | Must include inline reminder that downstream skill dispatch steps must use task-worker | Gap point identified in origin incident; orchestrators have no reminder before entering add-new-feature workflow | MEDIUM |\\n| groom-backlog-item/groomer-agent.md:32,73 | Dispatches `subagent_type: \\\"dh:backlog-item-groomer\\\"` directly in both Single Item and Multiple Items templates | Design intent requires clarification: either (a) groomer is an intentional exemption (must be documented inline with justification), or (b) must dispatch `subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\"` with agent field selecting groomer | Contentious case: groomer is not a SAM task executor (grooming workflow, not task execution) but dispatch pattern rule states \\\"every agent dispatch \u2014 no exceptions\\\"; exemption status undocumented | MEDIUM |\\n| CLAUDE.md:189-220 | Dispatch Pattern rule defined only in top-level Extension Rules section | Rule must be inlined at every dispatch step across all skills and reference files; top-level preamble insufficient | Centralized rule location permits orchestrators to silently skip it when following a specific skill; inlining ensures discipline at point of action | MEDIUM |\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n7 divergences found: 4 functional, 3 cosmetic\\n\\n**Functional divergences:**\\n- forensic-review/SKILL.md: direct `dh:code-reviewer` dispatch loses SAM MCP permissions\\n- dispatch/SKILL.md: missing `subagent_type` creates ambiguity in agent routing\\n- add-new-feature/SKILL.md: 6 delegation prompts name specialists without task-worker dispatch reminder\\n\\n**Cosmetic divergences:**\\n- execution/SKILL.md: unclear language permits wrong interpretation\\n- work-backlog-item/plan.md: missing inline reminder at workflow entry point\\n- groom-backlog-item/groomer-agent.md: undocumented exemption status\\n- CLAUDE.md: rule location does not match design intent (centralized vs inlined)\\n\\n**Note:** 9 of 18 identified dispatch sites in the codebase are already compliant with the design intent (implement-feature, complete-implementation, close/start, groom/error, groom/groom-drift, drift-check, role-resolution-protocol line 102, and 2 others). The work focuses on the 5 violations and 1 contentious case; the 7 compliant sites remain aligned.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **inline dh:task-worker dispatch discipline into every skill's dispatch step (remove claude.md-only rule)** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session on #1770 (2026-04-13) \u2014 orchestrator dispatched specialists directly because the skill being followed had no inline dispatch discipline\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-13\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:e7ae8316442eafd9adc498df7ce8b52a -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-13)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nRun the following grep commands to observe the violation and framing-wrong sites without making any changes:\\n\\n**1 actual violation \u2014 external agent dispatched directly inside a Mermaid node:**\\n\\n```bash\\ngrep -En \\\"agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst\\\" \\\\\\n  plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md\\n```\\n\\nExpected: line 115 contains a Mermaid node dispatching `agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst` directly as `subagent_type` without wrapping via `dh:task-worker`.\\n\\n**Framing-wrong site 1 \u2014 CLAUDE.md overstates task-worker as universal dispatcher:**\\n\\n```bash\\ngrep -n \\\"universal dispatch\\\\|must never be dispatched\\\\|always dispatch task-worker\\\\|every.*dispatch.*task-worker\\\" \\\\\\n  plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md\\n```\\n\\nExpected: lines 189-221 contain language asserting task-worker as a universal requirement for all dispatches, including named `dh:*` specialists.\\n\\n**Framing-wrong site 2 \u2014 role-resolution-protocol.md overstates task-worker universality:**\\n\\n```bash\\ngrep -n \\\"task-worker\\\" \\\\\\n  plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/role-resolution-protocol.md\\n```\\n\\nExpected: line 102 contains a paragraph asserting task-worker wrapping applies universally rather than scoped to SAM role-resolution context.\\n\\n**Ambiguous site 1 \u2014 swarm-task-planner agent-assignment table:**\\n\\n```bash\\ngrep -n \\\"task-worker\\\" \\\\\\n  plugins/development-harness/agents/swarm-task-planner.md | head -20\\n```\\n\\nExpected: lines 425-430 contain an agent-assignment table that does not distinguish external agents (profile_load targets) from named `dh:*` specialists (direct dispatch targets).\\n\\n**Ambiguous site 2 \u2014 implement-feature SKILL.md unqualified rule:**\\n\\n```bash\\ngrep -n \\\"always dispatch task-worker\\\\|always use task-worker\\\\|task-worker\\\" \\\\\\n  plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md | head -20\\n```\\n\\nExpected: line 80 contains an unqualified \\\"always dispatch task-worker\\\" or similar statement that does not scope the rule to external agents and generic workers.\\n\\n**Confirm no-op for the 11 correct direct-dispatch sites:**\\n\\n```bash\\ngrep -rn \\\"subagent_type.*dh:\\\" \\\\\\n  plugins/development-harness/ | grep -v \\\"task-worker\\\" | head -20\\n```\\n\\nExpected: matches are named `dh:*` specialists dispatched directly \u2014 these are correct and require no change.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n5/10 \u2014 P2 (narrower than prior grooming).\\n\\nPrior grooming estimated 5 violations and rated this P1. Wave 2 fact-check reduced that to 1 actual violation, 2 framing-wrong sites, and 2 ambiguous sites. The real-world blast radius is smaller: the only live violation is a single Mermaid node in `implement-feature/SKILL.md`. The risk of silent SAM-state-update loss exists but affects only the specific `agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst` dispatch path today. The CI guard does not yet exist, so the pattern can be reintroduced without detection \u2014 that gap justifies staying P2 rather than P3, but the lower violation count does not support P1.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- **Blocks**: Any SAM workflow that routes through `implement-feature/SKILL.md` line 115 and invokes `agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst` directly. That agent lacks SAM MCP and backlog MCP access, so task-state updates from that dispatch are silently dropped \u2014 the SAM plan shows the task as stuck or unclaimed while the external agent has already acted on it.\\n- **Bottleneck**: CI has no guard against this pattern. Any future edit that adds a new external-agent direct dispatch produces silent data loss in SAM state \u2014 no lint, no test, no warning surfaces the problem until a human observes a stuck task.\\n- **Framing wrong sites amplify risk**: The false \\\"universal dispatcher\\\" rule in `CLAUDE.md` lines 189-221 actively misleads orchestrators into believing direct dispatch of named `dh:*` specialists is also forbidden \u2014 or conversely, once an engineer reads the real rule elsewhere, they may doubt the CLAUDE.md authority and ignore both. Either failure mode degrades rule adherence.\\n- **No impact on the 11 correct direct-dispatch sites**: Named `dh:*` specialists dispatched directly are correct today and carry no silent-failure risk.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- **Silent failure eliminated at the 1 known violation site**: After the fix, `agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst` is dispatched via `dh:task-worker`, which carries SAM MCP and backlog MCP access \u2014 task-state updates reach the plan correctly.\\n- **CI guard prevents recurrence**: The new test file catches future external-agent direct dispatches automatically, converting a silent runtime failure mode into a visible CI failure at commit time.\\n- **CLAUDE.md rewrite removes the false universality claim**: Orchestrators reading the updated section get the accurate narrower rule \u2014 task-worker wraps external agents and generic workers; named `dh:*` specialists dispatch directly. No more ambiguity about whether the rule applies to `dh:planning`, `dh:code-review-python`, etc.\\n- **Inline reminders at the 2 ambiguous sites reduce cognitive load**: Engineers don't need to cross-reference CLAUDE.md to know which path applies at a dispatch step \u2014 the reminder is colocated with the action.\\n- **Positive framing removes unnecessary friction for correct patterns**: The 11 correct direct-dispatch sites are affirmed rather than implicitly threatened by the old \\\"never dispatch directly\\\" language.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAfter the fix, an orchestrator following any dispatch step in `plugins/development-harness/` observes the following:\\n\\n- When the dispatch step targets a named `dh:*` specialist (e.g., `dh:planning`, `dh:code-review-python`,