s positive framing, annotates reasons, and has descriptive headings. (Observable by reading the updated section and comparing against the checklist criteria \u2014 no tooling required beyond `cat`.)\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Skill | `/plugin-creator:prompt-optimization` \u2014 checklist for positive framing, reason annotation, descriptive headings; apply to the CLAUDE.md rewrite |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` lines 189-221 \u2014 the authoritative location of the current (wrong) rule; the rewrite target |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/role-resolution-protocol.md` line 102 \u2014 framing-wrong paragraph; use surrounding SAM context as scope anchor for the corrected text |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/agents/swarm-task-planner.md` lines 425-430 \u2014 agent-assignment table; existing table structure is the template for the clarifying note |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` line 80 \u2014 inline rule; existing sentence is the template for the qualified replacement |\\n| Canonical form | Inline reminder wording decided during RT-ICA Final Pass (reproduced in item description) \u2014 use verbatim when adding reminders at dispatch sites |\\n| Canonical form | CI-guard regex: `subagent_type\\\\s*=\\\\s*[\\\"'](?!dh:|general-purpose\\\\b)[a-z0-9_-]+:[a-z0-9_-]+[\\\"']` \u2014 must appear verbatim in the new test file |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n**Violation (requires fix):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 line 115: Mermaid node dispatches `agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst` directly\\n\\n**Framing-wrong (requires rewrite):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 lines 189-221: \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section overstates task-worker as universal dispatcher\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/role-resolution-protocol.md` \u2014 line 102: paragraph overstates task-worker universality outside SAM role-resolution context\\n\\n**Ambiguous (requires inline clarification):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/swarm-task-planner.md` \u2014 lines 425-430: agent-assignment table lacks note distinguishing external agents from `dh:*` specialists\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 line 80: unqualified \\\"always dispatch task-worker\\\" rule\\n\\n**To be created:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_dispatch_discipline*.py` \u2014 new test file implementing the CI-guard grep pattern\\n\\n**Correct \u2014 no change needed (11 sites):**\\nAll files containing `subagent_type.*dh:` patterns that dispatch named `dh:*` specialists directly. These are confirmed correct by Wave 2 fact-check and must not be touched.\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\nNo blockers. No downstream items blocked. Related item #1815 (task-worker.md frontmatter tools field) was opened during regroom as a suspected sibling, then closed wontfix 2026-04-14 after confirming the absent `tools:` field is intentional universal-worker configuration, not a defect \u2014 see RT-ICA condition 6.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall-Medium \u2014 narrower than the prior Medium estimate.\\n\\nWave 2 fact-check reduced the violation count from 5 to 1 actual violation. The remaining 4 sites are framing-wrong or ambiguous, requiring rewording rather than structural changes. The new test file is a single-pass grep-based test against known file paths and a canonical regex pattern. No new skill files, no schema changes, no MCP modifications. Work is concentrated in 5 existing files plus 1 new test file.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Backlog item #1770 (the session where this gap was first observed)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:80` (the single known inline occurrence today)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:80` is the ONE place where the rule is stated inline at a dispatch step: \\\"Always dispatch `dh:task-worker` as the `subagent_type`. The `agent:` field in the task YAML is NOT a routing directive for the orchestrator \u2014 it is read internally by `task-worker` via the SAM MCP (`sam_task` action) and passed to `profile_load` to specialize `task-worker`'s behavior.\\\"\\n\\nThe following skills perform dispatch or hand off to skills that dispatch, and do NOT inline the task-worker rule at their delegation step:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/plan.md` Step 4.2 \u2014 invokes `Skill(skill: \\\"add-new-feature\\\", args: \\\"{composed feature request}\\\")` without any dispatch-discipline reminder.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` Phase 1 through Phase 4 \u2014 names specialist agents (`@dh:feature-researcher`, `@dh:codebase-analyzer`, design-spec role, `@dh:swarm-task-planner`) in the delegation prompts, with no inline instruction to dispatch them via `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'`.\\n- Likely more (full audit pending) \u2014 `dh:dispatch`, `dh:execution`, `dh:task-decomposition`, `dh:forensic-review`, `dh:final-verification`, `dh:discovery`, `dh:planning`, `dh:context-integration`, `dh:complete-implementation`, and any other skill that delegates to an agent.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dispatch Site Audit Results\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe audit covered all dispatch `subagent_type=` call sites across `plugins/development-harness/skills/**/*.md`, `plugins/development-harness/skills/**/references/**/*.md`, and `plugins/development-harness/agents/**/*.md`. All sites were evaluated against the corrected premise:\\n- Named `dh:*` specialists dispatched directly = CORRECT\\n- External (non-`dh:`) agents dispatched without `dh:task-worker` wrapping = VIOLATION\\n- `dh:task-worker` for SAM tasks or external-agent wrapping = CORRECT\\n\\n### Dispatch Site Inventory\\n\\n| File | Line | Target | Category | Rationale |\\n|------|------|--------|----------|-----------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` | 64, 73 | `dh:code-reviewer` | CORRECT | Named dh specialist; direct dispatch is correct behavior |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | 115 | `agentskill-kaizen:transcript-analyst` | **VIOLATION** | External plugin agent dispatched directly without `dh:task-worker` wrapping |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | 159 | `dh:contract-verification` | CORRECT | Named dh specialist; direct dispatch is correct behavior |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | 80 | `dh:task-worker` | CORRECT | SAM task dispatch via task-worker is the correct pattern |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | 614 | `dh:task-worker` | CORRECT | SAM fix-cycle task dispatch via task-worker |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/references/groomer-agent.md` | 32, 73 | `dh:backlog-item-groomer` | CORRECT | Named dh specialist; direct dispatch is correct behavior |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/references/drift-check.md` | 11, 70 | `dh:task-worker` | CORRECT | Haiku-model task-worker dispatch for drift checking |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` | 236 | `dh:backlog-item-groomer` | CORRECT | Named dh specialist; direct dispatch is correct behavior |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/groom-drift.md` | 36, 78 | `dh:task-worker` | CORRECT | Haiku-model task-worker dispatch |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` | 18 | `dh:task-worker` | CORRECT | Error-recovery task dispatch via task-worker |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` | 118 | `dh:task-worker` | CORRECT | AC verification dispatch via task-worker |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` | 207, 218-219 | (rule text, not dispatch) | **FRAMING-WRONG** | \\\"universal dispatch agent for all dh workflows \u2014 no exceptions\\\" is incorrect; named dh specialists are dispatched directly |\\n\\n### TeamCreate Dispatch Sites (AMBIGUOUS \u2014 no explicit `subagent_type=`)\\n\\n| File | Location | Pattern | Category | Rationale |\\n|------|----------|---------|----------|-----------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` | Lines 9-31 | Grooming swarm teammates dispatched by name (impact-analyst, fact-checker, etc.) \u2014 no explicit `subagent_type=` shown | AMBIGUOUS | When orchestrator spawns these teammates via TeamCreate, they are dh specialists \u2014 correct to dispatch directly. But the doc omits the reminder that external-plugin agents appearing in `swarm.md` would need wrapping. Low risk because all teammates listed are dh agents. |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/agents/swarm-task-planner.md` | Lines 425-430 | Agent Assignment Rules table lists `python-engineering:python-cli-architect`, `python-engineering:python-pytest-architect`, `plugin-creator:agent-creator`, `plugin-creator:subagent-refactorer` as task `agent:` field values | AMBIGUOUS | These appear in the `agent:` field of SAM task YAML (read by task-worker via profile_load), NOT as `subagent_type=` dispatch targets. This is the correct pattern \u2014 task-worker wraps them. The assignment table itself is not a violation; it documents which external agents task-worker should load. The risk is that a future orchestrator misreads the table as a direct dispatch list. |\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Non Goals\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n- Removing the `dh:task-worker` dispatch rule\\n- Moving the rule to a different central location (e.g. a skills-meta doc) \u2014 the fix is INLINING at every dispatch step, not relocating the central rule\\n- Rewriting skills that already have the discipline inlined (only `implement-feature/SKILL.md:80` is known to have it today, but a full audit may surface more)\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n- Removing the `dh:task-worker` dispatch rule\\n- Moving the rule to a different central location (e.g. a skills-meta doc) \u2014 the fix is INLINING at every dispatch step, not relocating the central rule\\n- Rewriting skills that already have the discipline inlined (only `implement-feature/SKILL.md:80` is known to have it today, but a full audit may surface more)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEvery dispatch step in every dh skill, reference file, workflow document, and agent prompt contains an inline reminder \u2014 at the step, not in a top-level preamble \u2014 that the dispatch target MUST be `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'` and the specialist is selected by the `agent:` field in the task YAML (read internally by task-worker via `profile_load`). Orchestrators following any skill step should not need to remember a CLAUDE.md rule to dispatch correctly.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe `dh:task-worker` dispatch discipline \u2014 \\\"every agent dispatch in a dh workflow MUST go through `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'`, never a specialist directly\\\" \u2014 currently lives in `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` under the \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section. The rule is not inlined into the skills that actually perform dispatch, so any orchestrator following a skill can silently violate it by delegating directly to a specialist agent at a delegation step.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Regroom 2026 04 14 Corrected Premise Authoritative\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nPrior grooming (2026-04-13) was built on a wrong premise and its Impact Radius, Fact-Check verdicts, Design Intent Alignment assessment, and Acceptance Criteria are NOT usable. Discard them.\\n\\n### Corrected `dh:task-worker` purpose\\n\\n`dh:task-worker` exists for exactly two cases:\\n\\n1. **Agentic tasks where no named `dh:*` specialist exists** \u2014 task-worker is the generic worker that loads specialist behavior dynamically via `profile_load` from the SAM task YAML's `agent:` field.\\n2. **Bringing in external agents from OTHER plugins/systems** (e.g. `python-engineering:python-pytest-architect`, `plugin-creator:subagent-refactorer`) as workers inside the SAM lifecycle. task-worker wraps them so they get SAM MCP access, backlog MCP access, and task-state updates.\\n\\n**Named `dh:*` specialists are dispatched DIRECTLY** \u2014 NOT routed through task-worker. Direct dispatch of a dh specialist is CORRECT behavior, not a violation. Examples of correct direct dispatch:\\n\\n- `dh:backlog-item-groomer` (in groomer-agent.md)\\n- `dh:code-reviewer` (in forensic-review/SKILL.md)\\n- `dh:feature-researcher`, `dh:codebase-analyzer`, `dh:swarm-task-planner`, `dh:plan-validator`, `dh:dh-context-gathering` (in add-new-feature/SKILL.md phases)\\n- `dh:impact-analyst`, `dh:fact-checker`, `dh:rtica-assessor`, `dh:alignment-analyst`, `dh:classifier` (in groom swarm.md)\\n\\n### The actual #1770 violation\\n\\nThe orchestrator in session #1770 dispatched `subagent_type=\\\"python-engineering:python-pytest-architect\\\"` and `subagent_type=\\\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\\\"` directly from within a SAM task execution context. These are EXTERNAL agents from another plugin. Under the corrected premise, they should have been routed through `dh:task-worker` \u2014 not because \\\"all dispatches must go through task-worker\\\", but because external-agent dispatches specifically require the task-worker wrapper for SAM lifecycle integration.\\n\\n### What the regroom swarm must produce\\n\\n- **Impact Radius**: enumerate only dispatch sites that route EXTERNAL agents (non-`dh:*`) directly, plus any dh specialist dispatches that bypass an expected SAM task lifecycle. Do NOT list direct dispatches of named `dh:*` specialists as violations.\\n- **Fact-Check**: verify which external agents are referenced in dh skills/agents/workflow files. Verify the `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section currently states the overly broad \\\"universal dispatcher\\\" rule.\\n- **Design Intent Alignment**: redo against the corrected narrower rule.\\n- **Issue Classification**: keep `missing-guardrail` \u2014 unchanged.\\n- **Priority**: keep P2 Medium \u2014 unchanged.\\n- **CLAUDE.md rewrite scope**: the existing \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section itself needs correction \u2014 it currently states \\\"`dh:task-worker` is the universal dispatch agent for all dh workflows\\\" and \\\"`general-purpose` must never be dispatched from any dh skill or extension point\\\" \u2014 both framings are wrong under the corrected premise. Rewrite with the narrower rule: task-worker wraps external agents and generic workers; named dh specialists are dispatched directly.\\n- **Canonical inline reminder phrasing**: must encode the narrower rule (e.g. \\\"Dispatching an external agent? Wrap via `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'` with the agent name in the SAM task `agent:` field. Reason: task-worker provides SAM lifecycle + MCP access to workers that don't have it natively.\\\")\\n- **CI guard grep pattern**: match external-agent direct-dispatch shape only (e.g. `subagent_type=['\\\"](?!dh:|general-purpose)[a-z0-9-]+:` \u2014 any non-dh plugin-namespaced agent dispatched directly), not direct dispatch of dh specialists.\\n\\n### Prerequisite skills for swarm agents\\n\\nBefore analysis, every swarm agent should have these loaded as context:\\n\\n- `plugin-creator:memory-and-rules` (memory hierarchy \u2014 plugin-internal CLAUDE.md is developer-facing only)\\n- `plugin-creator:prompt-optimization` (positive framing + `Reason:` annotations)\\n- `plugin-creator:claude-plugins-reference-2026` (plugin.json components; CLAUDE.md is not a component)\\n- `plugin-creator:claude-skills-overview-2026` (skill loading; runtime context is per-skill)\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nPrior grooming (2026-04-13) was built on a wrong premise and its Impact Radius, Fact-Check verdicts, Design Intent Alignment assessment, and Acceptance Criteria are NOT usable. Discard them.\\n\\n### Corrected `dh:task-worker` purpose\\n\\n`dh:task-worker` exists for exactly two cases:\\n\\n1. **Agentic tasks where no named `dh:*` specialist exists** \u2014 task-worker is the generic worker that loads specialist behavior dynamically via `profile_load` from the SAM task YAML's `agent:` field.\\n2. **Bringing in external agents from OTHER plugins/systems** (e.g. `python-engineering:python-pytest-architect`, `plugin-creator:subagent-refactorer`) as workers inside the SAM lifecycle. task-worker wraps them so they get SAM MCP access, backlog MCP access, and task-state updates.\\n\\n**Named `dh:*` specialists are dispatched DIRECTLY** \u2014 NOT routed through task-worker. Direct dispatch of a dh specialist is CORRECT behavior, not a violation. Examples of correct direct dispatch:\\n\\n- `dh:backlog-item-groomer` (in groomer-agent.md)\\n- `dh:code-reviewer` (in forensic-review/SKILL.md)\\n- `dh:feature-researcher`, `dh:codebase-analyzer`, `dh:swarm-task-planner`, `dh:plan-validator`, `dh:dh-context-gathering` (in add-new-feature/SKILL.md phases)\\n- `dh:impact-analyst`, `dh:fact-checker`, `dh:rtica-assessor`, `dh:alignment-analyst`, `dh:classifier` (in groom swarm.md)\\n\\n### The actual #1770 violation\\n\\nThe orchestrator in session #1770 dispatched `subagent_type=\\\"python-engineering:python-pytest-architect\\\"` and `subagent_type=\\\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\\\"` directly from within a SAM task execution context. These are EXTERNAL agents from another plugin. Under the corrected premise, they should have been routed through `dh:task-worker` \u2014 not because \\\"all dispatches must go through task-worker\\\", but because external-agent dispatches specifically require the task-worker wrapper for SAM lifecycle integration.\\n\\n### What the regroom swarm must produce\\n\\n- **Impact Radius**: enumerate only dispatch sites that route EXTERNAL agents (non-`dh:*`) directly, plus any dh specialist dispatches that bypass an expected SAM task lifecycle. Do NOT list direct dispatches of named `dh:*` specialists as violations.\\n- **Fact-Check**: verify which external agents are referenced in dh skills/agents/workflow files. Verify the `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section currently states the overly broad \\\"universal dispatcher\\\" rule.\\n- **Design Intent Alignment**: redo against the corrected narrower rule.\\n- **Issue Classification**: keep `missing-guardrail` \u2014 unchanged.\\n- **Priority**: keep P2 Medium \u2014 unchanged.\\n- **CLAUDE.md rewrite scope**: the existing \\\"Dispatch Pattern (Extension Rules)\\\" section itself needs correction \u2014 it currently states \\\"`dh:task-worker` is the universal dispatch agent for all dh workflows\\\" and \\\"`general-purpose` must never be dispatched from any dh skill or extension point\\\" \u2014 both framings are wrong under the corrected premise. Rewrite with the narrower rule: task-worker wraps external agents and generic workers; named dh specialists are dispatched directly.\\n- **Canonical inline reminder phrasing**: must encode the narrower rule (e.g. \\\"Dispatching an external agent? Wrap via `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'` with the agent name in the SAM task `agent:` field. Reason: task-worker provides SAM lifecycle + MCP access to workers that don't have it natively